An embodiment provides an apparatus that evaluates body composition and hydration by measuring a human subject’s resistance at zero frequency and at infinite frequency. The apparatus includes methods for indirectly determining extracellular water mass, intracellular water mass, lean body mass, and body fat mass; tracking daily variations in each of these quantities; identifying acute changes in extracellular and intracellular water mass; and individually calibrating these indirect measurements.

The embodiment also includes a method that fits mathematical models to sequential indirect measurements of lean body mass and fat mass. Using minimum-variance estimation and prediction, it enables dynamic, individualized, indirect determination of:

- daily body-composition changes, expressed as changes in glycogen, fat, and protein stores;
- daily utilized macronutrient energy intake, including utilized calories from carbohydrates, fats, and proteins;
- daily macronutrient oxidation rates for carbohydrates, fats, and proteins;
- daily resting metabolic rate;
- daily unidentified energy losses or gains;
- daily endogenous lipolysis rate;
- daily nitrogen excretion;
- daily gluconeogenesis from protein;
- daily extracellular water mass;
- daily intracellular water mass; and
- acute changes in extracellular and intracellular water mass.
